WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.110
Earlier civilizations had different and
arguably more sophisticated perceptions

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:10.290
of time. In most pre-modern cultures the
past hovered over the present. Ancestors

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.590
were felt to be watching. Past and future
generations were understood to be

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:18.270
knitted into one fabric. The medieval
Scandinavian worldview included the

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:23.460
concept of 'wyrd,' loosely fate, or the
power of the past to shape the present

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:29.490
and future. Wyrd was embodied in the
world tree Yggdrasil, which gives

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:33.450
structure to all of time and space. It
was maintained by the mysterious female

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.030
Norns
who water it from an ancient and

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:40.440
continuously replenishing well while
reciting the orlog,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.969
the eternal laws that hold
up the world. In Ghanaian tradition

00:00:44.969 --> 00:00:48.270
there's the concept of 'Sankofa,'
usually symbolized by a backward-looking

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:53.100
bird and associated with the proverb: "It
is not wrong to go back for that which

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.000
you have forgotten." In other words: move
forward but remember the past.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.129
There's the Buddhist concept of 'Sati,'
usually translated as mindfulness, but

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:06.150
actually something closer to memory of
the present, suggesting a subtler

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:10.080
relationship with time. In these
traditions and many others, the past

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.360
present and future coexist and interact
in ways much more nuanced than our

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:21.900
simplistic linear view of time. In our
culture, we tend to mistake technological

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.610
prowess for wisdom. The people we call
visionaries base their conceptions of

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.210
the future on the notion that we should
do everything in our power to circumvent

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.570
the irksome constraints of the natural
world that have shackled hapless

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:38.100
denizens of the past—not appreciating
that earth is so much more powerful and

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.500
more patient than we are, and will always
prevail. The idea that earth time just

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.560
doesn't apply to us leads to the
delusion that we can outsmart the

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.750
complex system of biogeochemical cycles
and, for example, magically solve our

00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.890
climate woes through stratospheric sulfate
injection, and expect that there won't be

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:59.820
a powerful backlash of unintended
consequences as with contra dancers

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:05.250
reorganize themselves. Disrespect for
time also seduces us into thinking that

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:09.880
we could skip over all that inefficient
of years of coevolution stuff and just

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:15.430
terraform ourselves another planet.
Engineering the climate or terraforming

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.270
Mars sound easy if you're completely
unaware of the intrinsic timescales of

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:23.740
geological and biological phenomena, the
deep evolutionary pathways that gave

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:27.100
rise to the world we live in, the
intricately choreographed

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.510
behind-the-scenes biogeochemical cycles,
the housekeeping crew that make earth

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:36.580
habitable. Such ideas are beyond hubris,
revealing not only a profound ignorance

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:40.510
of the earth but also of human nature.
What makes us think we could

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:44.320
successfully change the rules that
govern this or another planet when we

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.700
haven't even learned to govern ourselves?

